2 CONT The Five Mythical Emperors (Wudi) 五帝 
2 CONT
2 CONT name residence
2 CONT
2 CONT (reign time) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di (Huangdi), the Yellow Emperor 黃帝, surname Xuanyuan 軒轅氏 Youxiong 有熊 (100 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Zhi 帝摯, surname Shaohao 少昊氏, called Qing 清)
2 CONT
2 CONT This person is not by all historians accepted as one of the mythical emperors. Xifang 西方 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuan Xu (Zhuanxu) 顓頊 , surname Gaoyang 高陽氏 Pu 濮 (78 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Di Ku (Diku) 帝嚳, surname Gaoxin 高辛氏 Bo 亳 (63 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yao 帝堯, surname Tang 唐 or Taotang 陶唐氏, called Fangxun 放勛 Ji 冀 (100 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shun 帝舜, surname Yu 虞 or Youyu 有虞氏, called Chonghua 重華 Ji (50 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Old Chinese sources often speak of the "Three Augusts" and "Five Emperors" (Sanhuang Wudi 三皇五帝), but scholars disagree who these persons are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Three Augusts (Sanhuang 三皇) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fu Xi (Fuxi) 伏羲 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Nong (Shennong) 神農 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di (Huangdi) 黃帝, Nü Wa (Nüwa) 女媧, Sui Ren (Suiren) 燧人, Zhu Rong (Zhurong) 祝融 or Gong Gong (Gonggong) 共工 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Five Emperors (Wudi 五帝), different versions 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shao Hao (Shaohao) 少昊 or Huang Di, Gao Yang (Gaoyang) 高陽=Zhuan Xu (Zhuanxu) 顓頊, Gao Xin (Gaoxin) 高辛=Di Ku (Diku) 帝嚳, Yao 堯 and Shun 舜 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tai Hao (Taihao) 太昊, Yan Di (Yandi) 炎帝, Huang Di, Shao Hao and Zhuan Xu 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mi Xi (Mixi) 宓戯 (or 宓戲)=Fu Xi, Shen Nong, Huang Di, Yao and Shun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Huang Di, Shao Hao, Di Ku, Yao, Shun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuan Xu (Gaoyang) is said to be a grandson of the Yellow Emperor (his father being Chang Yi [Changyi] 昌意), Di Ku a great-grandson of the Yellow Emperor (his father being Jiao Ji [Jiaoji] 蟜極, his grand-father being Xuan Ao [Xuan'a
2 CONC o] 玄囂), Yao a son of Di Ku, and Shun a descendant of Zhuan Xu (his father being Gu Sou [Gusou] 瞽叟, his grandfather Jiao Niu [Jiaoniu] 蟜牛, his great-grandfather Juwang [Juwang] or Gou Wang [Gouwang] 句望, whose father was Jing Kang [
2 CONC Jingkang] 敬康, his father Qiong Chan [Qiongchan] 窮蟬, his father Zhuan Xu). 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://history.cultural-china.com/en/46History3800.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Zhuan Xu was a son of Chang Yi and grand-son of the Yellow Emperor. He resided in Diqiu or Pu, his surname of Gaoyang (might also be a place name). He is said to have deepened the knowledge of calendar and astronomy, used 
2 CONC ghosts and spirits for assistance in his government - over a realm that covered the surface of modern China, thought the people by refining the universal breath and installed regulated rituals and sacrifices. One of his sons was Q
2 CONC iong Chan (also called Qiong Xi), ancestor of Emperor Shun.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuanxu
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuanxu (simplified Chinese: 颛顼; traditional Chinese: 顓頊; pinyin: Zhuānxū), also known as Gaoyang (高陽) is a monarch of ancient China.
2 CONT
2 CONT A grandson of the Yellow Emperor, Zhuanxu led the Shi clan in an eastward migration to present-day Shandong, where intermarriages with the Dongyi clan enlarged and augmented their tribal influences. At age twenty, he became their 
2 CONC sovereign, going on to rule for seventy-eight years until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He made contributions to a unified calendar, astrology, religion reforms to oppose shamanism, upheld the patriarchal (as opposed to the previous matriarchal) system, and forbade close-kin marriage. Zhuanxu is held by many to be on
2 CONC e of the Five Emperors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhuanxu was succeeded by his cousin's son, Ku. In the Shiji, he criticized one of his sons for being a dullard. Since only two sons were named, it might have been Gun, father of Yu the Great or Qiongchan, the ancestor of Shun. Yao
2 CONC  had also criticized Gun for being incompetent and ruinous. The Shiji labelled Qiongchan an insignificant commoner though it does not mention how he fell from grace. He also had eight unnamed sons of good repute that later worked 
2 CONC for Shun.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuanxu
