2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danzhu
2 CONT
2 CONT Dan Zhu (丹朱) was the son of Emperor Yao (Tang Yao). His mother was a concubine San Yi.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was not very intelligent, and spent most his time playing a game called Weiqi (go). Legend has it that when Emperor Yao wanted to replace Yihe (a Tiandi Official, equivalent to a prime minister) Dan Zhu was recommended for the 
2 CONC position but Emperor Yao disagreed, stating that Dan was not suitable at anything else other than Weiqi. In anticipation of Dan Zhu's anger, Yao exiled his son Dan Zhu to Yan (present day Zhucheng in Shandong), and appointed his o
2 CONC fficial Shun as his successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.yutopian.com/go/gowinds/yao.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Yao Taught Dan Zhu Weiqi
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Yao Di had a habit of touring his kingdom with the company of his administration and observed the need of his people. One day, he came to the Xuan Yuan Mountain of I County (Anhui) and was tired. Yao fell asleep and had a 
2 CONC dream. He dreamt of Emperor Huang Di playing a game of Weiqi against a fairy Yong Cheng. Yao had never seen this game before. Huang Di explained to him that he invented this game in preparation for a battle against the fierce trib
2 CONC al leader Chi You. Black and white stones represent soldiers from both sides. With this game, Huang Di defeated Chi You. Yao asked Huang Di to teach him the game. After he woke up, Yao reconstructed the rules based on his memory. 
2 CONC Because of this, Zhang Hua of Han Dynasty wrote in Bowuzhi, 'Yao created Weiqi'. Dan Zhu was the son of Yao's concubine San Yi. After Yao grew up, he loved to play and had no interest in studying. To enlighten Dan Zhu, Yao taught 
2 CONC Dan Zhu Weiqi. Due to Dan Zhu's playful nature, he was quick in learning the game and soon beat his father. Zhang Hua recorded in Bowuzhi, 'Dan Zhu was great in Weiqi.' Unfortunately, Dan has no interest in anything else besides W
2 CONC eiqi. When Yao was advanced in age he decided to elect a Tiandi Official to replace Yihe. His followers recommended Dan Zhu but Yau disagreed, stating that Dan was no good in anything else other than Weiqi. Yao appointed Shun as h
2 CONC is successor, giving his two daughters to Shun as wives. Eventually Dan Zhu was exiled to Yan (Yan Zhu Cheng of Shandong).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Danzhu''' - From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Danzhu''' (丹朱) was the son of the legendary Chinese Emperor Yao (Tang Yao). His mother was a concubine, San Yi.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was described as vicious and a playboy, and his father Yao is said to have invented the game of Weiqi to favorably influence him.[1] Various stories have him either banished, executed, or attempting (and failing) to kill his fa
2 CONC ther.[1] It was said that "those who had to try a lawsuit did not go to Danzhu, but to Shun."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sima Qian wrote that "Yao's son Danzhu and Shun's son Shangjun were allowed to have their own territories in order to offer sacrifices to their ancestors", while Qiao Zhou wrote that "Yao's son was given benefice at Tang 唐."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bamboo Annals represent Yao as having banished prince Danzhu to Danshui in his 58th year of his reign. They add that following Yao's abdication, Danzhu kept away from Shun, and that after the death of Yao, "Shun tried to yield
2 CONC  the throne to him, but in vain." It was at this point that Shun invested Danzhu with Tang. However, an alternative account elsewhere in the Annals holds that Shun dethroned and imprisoned Yao, raising Danzhu to the throne for a s
2 CONC hort time before seizing it himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the fact that Danzhu did not succeed his father in most historical accounts, Danzhu is referred to with the title 帝 (Di, Emperor) in Zhou writings.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danzhu
