1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yáo Zhòng Huá (姚重華) is another name for Emperor Shun (虞舜), a legendary ruler in ancient China. Based on the surrounding page content, he was born between 2478 BCE and 2058 BCE and was the son of Yú Gǔ Sǒu (虞槔, 瞽叟) 
2 CONC and Wò Dēng (握登). His family included multiple wives, Yī é Huáng (伊娥皇) and Dēng Bǐ (登比), as well as a daughter, Yáo Nǚ (姚女修).
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Significance
2 CONT Emperor Shun is one of the Five Emperors in Chinese mythology and history. He is celebrated for his virtue, wisdom, and leadership, succeeding Emperor Yao and continuing his legacy of benevolent governance. His reign emphasized mo
2 CONC ral integrity, social harmony, and effective administration, setting a precedent for future rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Lineage
2 CONT Father: Yú Gǔ Sǒu (虞槔, 瞽叟)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Wò Dēng (握登)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives: Yī é Huáng (伊娥皇), Dēng Bǐ (登比)
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter: Yáo Nǚ (姚女修)
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers: Yú Míng Shī (虞名失), Yú Xiàng (虞像, half-brother)
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT Emperor Shun is remembered for his filial piety, overcoming hardships imposed by his family to become a revered ruler. His governance laid the foundation for the Xia Dynasty, marking a transition from mythological rulers to histor
2 CONC ical dynasties.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Shun
