1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_Ling_of_Qin
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Ling of Qin (Chinese: 秦靈公; pinyin: Qín Líng Gōng; died 415 BC), personal name unknown, was from 424 to 415 BC the monarch of the Qin state.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Ling's predecessor was his grandfather Duke Huai. In 425 BC Qin general Chao (鼌) and other ministers attacked and besieged Duke Huai, and Duke Huai committed suicide. As Duke Huai's son Crown Prince Zhaozi (昭子) died early, th
2 CONC e ministers installed Duke Ling, Zhaozi's son, on the throne.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 419 BC, the sixth year of Duke Ling's reign, Qin attacked the state of Wei at the city of Shaoliang (少梁, in present-day Hancheng, Shaanxi).[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Ling reigned for 10 years and died in 415 BC. However, the throne was passed to his uncle Duke Jian, son of Duke Huai and younger brother of Zhaozi. Duke Ling's own son Shixi, later known as Duke Xian, was exiled to Wei. Duke
2 CONC  Xian would eventually ascend the throne nearly 30 years later, after Duke Jian's grandson Chuzi II was killed.[1][2]
