2 CONT The Muslim conquest of Persia :
2 CONT
2 CONT The Persian ruler Khosrau II (Parviz) defeated a dangerous rebellion within his own empire, the Bahram Chobin's rebellion. He afterward turned his energies towards his traditional Byzantine enemies, leading to the Byzantine-Sassan
2 CONC id War of 602–628. For a few years, he succeeded gloriously. From 612 to 622, he extended the Persian borders almost to the same extent that they were under the Achaemenid dynasty (550–330 BC), capturing Western states as far as E
2 CONC gypt, Palestine (the conquest of the latter being assisted by a Jewish army), and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantines regrouped and pushed back in 622 under Heraclius. Khosrau was defeated at the Battle of Nineveh in 627, and the Byzantines recaptured all of Syria and penetrated far into the Persian provinces of Mesopotamia. In 629
2 CONC , Khosrau's general Shahrbaraz agreed to peace, and the border between the two empires was once again the same as it was in 602.
2 CONT
2 CONT Khosrau II was executed in 628 and as a result, there were numerous claimants to the throne; from 628 to 632 there were ten kings and queens of Persia. The last, Yazdegerd III, was a grandson of Khosrau II and was said to be a mer
2 CONC e child aged 8 years
