1 NAME Xie 歇/光明/肃失 /Huáng 黄/
2 GIVN Xie 歇/光明/肃失
2 _MARNM Huáng 黄
1 NAME Huáng Xie 黄歇, 75G
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE -238
1 BIRT
2 DATE -314
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 江夏開基始祖歇公 314BC to 238BC
2 CONT
2 CONT Huáng Xiē (黄歇) was born in Korea (新罗). When he was 20 (in 295 BC) he returned to China to study and lived in Qiánzhōng (黔中) in the State of Chu (楚国) (present-day Chángdé city 常德市, Húnán province 湖南省).   
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning, he worked for the State of Jìn (晋国)*1 and was appointed to the position of Dàfū (大夫)*2.  Later, the State of Jìn (晋国) was wiped out by the joint forces of the States Hán (韩国), Zhào (赵国), Wèi (魏国).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Huáng Xiē (黄歇) had three thousand swordsmen wearing red boots under his command and was also heading a one-hundred-thousand soldier strong army.  He was also granted twelve counties of Huáiběi (淮北) (Note: north to the Huái River).
2 CONC   When King Yōu (幽王) of Chǔ succeeded the reign (in 237 B.C.), he was unfortunately framed-up by a treacherous Court Official named Lǐ Yuán (李园) and later died in the Kāi Yuán Temple (开元寺) of Cháng Dé (常德). Later he was buried in 
2 CONC the Rén Yì Village (仁义村) of Huáng Hè Township (黄鹤乡) of the Jiāng Xià County (江夏县), which was 30 li (Note: =15 kilometers) outside the City.  His tomb was located in the Qián (note: meaning male) Mountain (乾山) and facing direction 
2 CONC of “Xū” (戌)*5.   His wife and concubines were buried in the same grave, which faced “Xū” Mountain (戌山)*6 and in direction of Chén (辰)*7 with stone lions, elephants, tigers, horses and slaves in front.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Note *1: One of the stronger states that lasted till the period of “Warring States” (战国时代). Note *2: A senior official in feudal China Note *3: Chǔ (楚) was one of the strongest and longest lasting Kingdom States.  It was the last 
2 CONC one to be conquered by the Kingdom State of Qín (秦). Note *4: This was during the historic period called the “Warring States” when the seven strongest states – Hán (韩), Zhào (赵), Qí (齐), Jìn (晋), Yàn (燕), Qín (秦) and Chǔ (楚) - fou
2 CONC ght with each other for over two hundred years, finally the King of Qín (秦王) had conquered all the other states and unified China and became the First Emperor of Qín Dynasty ( 秦朝). Note *5: The 11th of the twelve Earthly Branches,
2 CONC  meaning the direction of Wind. Note *6: Meaning military Note *7: It is one of the twelve directions of the Chinese horoscope, indicating time 7:00-9:00am.
